# Principles Before Personalities: SEO plan

Last updated 2026-09-25 by SEO. The on-page work is done in `site/`. This file covers what happens off the page and at launch.

## Who we are trying to reach

People in Alcoholics Anonymous and other 12-step recovery who read and study the Big Book: newcomers getting their first copy, people working the steps with a sponsor, sponsors themselves, and members of Big Book study meetings at their home group. They mostly search in plain words, such as "Big Book app", "Big Book online", "read the Big Book", "1939 first edition Big Book", "Big Book page 58", "How It Works" or "Big Book study". No keyword tool was used, so there are no search-volume figures here. These phrases come from the product and from how people talk about the book.

## Trademark and tone rules (keep these for every page, post and listing)

- Use "Alcoholics Anonymous", "AA" and "the Big Book" only to describe what the app contains. Never in a way that suggests AA made, approved or recommends it.
- Keep the visible line "Not affiliated with or endorsed by Alcoholics Anonymous World Services, Inc." near the top of the page and in the footer.
- Always say "1939 first edition" and "public domain". Never imply it is the current fourth edition.
- Don't put AA's name or logo in the app name, icon, domain or social handles.
- No keyword stuffing, no fake reviews, no invented ratings.

## Launch-gated steps (in order)

1. **URL is set.** The site is live on Netlify at `https://principles-before-personalities.netlify.app/`, and canonical, og:url, images, JSON-LD, robots.txt and sitemap.xml all use it. If it later moves to a custom domain or GitHub Pages, swap the base URL with the command in `site/README.md` and set up a redirect from the old address.
2. **robots.txt.** Netlify serves it at the root, so it works as written. (If the site ever moves to a GitHub Pages project address like `USERNAME.github.io/REPO/`, crawlers ignore a robots.txt inside `REPO/`; that's harmless, and the sitemap can be submitted in Search Console directly.)
3. **Google Search Console.** Add a URL-prefix property for the exact final URL. Verify with the HTML-tag method (paste Google's `<meta name="google-site-verification">` into `index.html`'s `<head>`), since a netlify.app subdomain doesn't allow DNS verification. Submit `sitemap.xml`, then use URL Inspection on the home page and request indexing.
4. **Bing Webmaster Tools.** Import the property from Search Console. Bing also feeds DuckDuckGo and several AI assistants.
5. **Test the link preview.** Paste the URL into a Facebook sharing debugger and an iMessage/Slack message to confirm the 1200×630 image and title show up.
6. **Test structured data.** Run the page through Google's Rich Results Test and the Schema.org validator. Note that Google now shows FAQ rich results mainly for government and health sites, and SoftwareApplication stars need real ratings, so don't expect fancy search snippets. The markup still helps other engines and AI answers describe the app correctly.
7. **Downloads live.** Confirm all four buttons work, and that the iPhone button points to the hosted web app (for example `iphone/`) rather than a zip, then add that URL to `sitemap.xml`.

## Optional but worth considering (James decides)

- **A custom domain on Netlify** (for example a `.app` or `.org` name built around "principles before personalities"). A short memorable domain is easier to share by word of mouth, which is how this audience mostly finds things, and it lets you verify Search Console by DNS. Avoid "aa" or "alcoholics anonymous" in the domain.
- **A dedicated iPhone page** at `iphone/` with its own title and short instructions, once the web app is hosted there.

## Where recovery folks actually look (without spamming)

The recovery community is sensitive to promotion, and many spaces ban it outright. The approach is to be useful, honest and transparent, and to post only where self-promotion is allowed.

- **Reddit.** r/alcoholicsanonymous, r/stopdrinking and r/redditorsinrecovery have strict rules. Read each sidebar first. Where there is a weekly resources or self-promotion thread, post once there with a plain description and the disclaimer. Don't post links in people's personal threads.
- **Word of mouth.** The strongest channel. A one-line description plus the URL that James can share with his own sponsor, sponsees or friends in recovery, and that they can pass on if they like it. Do not hand out flyers at meetings or announce it in meetings, since that conflicts with AA's traditions and would hurt the project's reputation.
- **Recovery app roundups and blogs.** Some sober-living blogs and "best recovery apps" lists accept tips. A short, honest email offering a free, offline, no-account Big Book reader is a reasonable pitch. (Mail can draft these for James to approve.)
- **Product Hunt / Hacker News "Show HN".** A one-time launch post aimed at the privacy and offline angle, not at recovery groups.
- **AlternativeTo and similar directories.** List it as an alternative to other Big Book reader apps, described accurately.
- **GitHub itself.** If the code is public, a clear README and repository description linking to the site helps both search and trust.

## Not doing

- Posting in private recovery Facebook groups, Discord servers or meeting chats unless an admin invites it.
- Buying links, directory spam, or mass-posting the same message.
- Using AA's name in ads or in a way that implies endorsement.

## After launch

- Check Search Console after two to four weeks for which queries show impressions, and adjust the title, description and FAQ wording to match the words people actually use.
- If a phrase like "Big Book page numbers" or "1939 Big Book" earns impressions, consider a short, genuinely useful page on that topic (for example a chapter-and-page index of the 1939 text), rather than thin keyword pages.
